#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Seconds-scale static pre-flight for a full-suite round trip (#529 S5 §5).

Eight checks, in order:

  1. Compile every Python file in the REPOSITORY — not bin/ alone (#655) —
     without writing bytecode. Files are selected by `.py` extension OR by a
     Python shebang: bin/cctally is EXTENSIONLESS, so an extension-keyed scan
     would skip the most important file in the tree, and
     bin/codex-session-metrics shows that is a class rather than one special
     case. The subject set comes from the SAME pruned walk check 2 uses, so a
     Python syntax error outside bin/ is now found rather than invisible; the
     two checks were asymmetric from #641 until #655 closed it.
  2. Syntax-check every shell subject in the REPOSITORY — not bin/ alone — on
     three legs. The primary parse uses the interpreter chosen per file from
     its own shebang rather than assumed. A non-empty stderr from that parse is
     a finding even at exit 0, which is how bash 5 reports an unterminated
     here-document and how eleven of them went unreported for months. And every
     subject whose interpreter is bash is parsed a second time by a bash 3.x
     interpreter, because 3.2 is the floor this repository declares while
     `#!/usr/bin/env bash` resolves to Homebrew 5.x on every box that runs the
     suite, so the declared floor was never actually exercised. The subject set
     is a PRUNED filesystem walk rather than `git ls-files`, because this tool
     must not require a git repository: the tree it checks may be a
     materialized copy. See check_shell and iter_shell_subjects.
  3. Invoke bin/cctally-test-owners --verify.
  4. Validate tests/authoritative-harness-durations.tsv against the estate
     manifest, through the SAME validator the dispatch scheduler applies. The
     scheduler treats a bad table as a non-fatal fallback, because a stale
     table must never fail a gate; this is where it is refused instead, at the
     keyboard rather than sixteen minutes later.
  5. Ask bin/cctally-test-all's side-effect-free plan mode for the dispatch
     decision it would take, and require `scheduler=lpt`. Check 4 reads the
     same three files the scheduler reads and would pass while the scheduler
     still fell back at run time: python3 absent or answering non-zero,
     _lib_harness_durations unimportable from <root>/bin, or a broken
     shell-side capture are all silent fallbacks no file validation can see.
     A tree carrying no aggregator has no plan to report and is not faulted.
  6. Classify every manifest row's harness path against .mirror-allowlist and
     compare it with the declared visibility. Private profile only. The
     authoritative admission check on the runner is unchanged; this is
     duplicate LOCAL coverage of a check that reads three files and executes
     nothing, and today costs a full round trip to discover.
  7. Check CHANGELOG.md against the user-facing entry policy in
     docs/changelog-policy.md, through the same pure kernel and the same two
     path predicates bin/cctally-changelog-lint applies. The file publishes to
     the public repository verbatim and is reused as a release-note body and a
     mirror commit message, so a non-compliant entry is an outward-facing
     defect; catching it here costs a parse of one file.
  8. Validate tests/authoritative-runtime-budget.json through the REAL
     contract_budget_load, invoked as a caught subprocess. That loader is
     embedded Python inside bin/_lib-test-contract.sh, so unlike check 4 there
     is no module to import and no Python API to call; running the shell
     function is what keeps the committed file validated by the parser that
     refuses it at admission and keeps the refusal vocabulary in one place.
     A malformed budget otherwise costs a full round trip to discover, because
     admission is where it is caught today.

Checks 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 are admitted under one boundary rule: pre-flight takes only
checks that parse or verify local files deterministically and that have
identical coverage inside the suite. No pytest, no harness execution, no
fixture regeneration, no network probe, no runner-capability query, and no
performance measurement. Check 5 is inside that rule rather than an exception
to it: plan mode is the aggregator's documented side-effect-free dry run, it
exits before admission and before any harness launch, and the suite reaches
the same decision through tests/test_test_all_scheduler.py.

Exit 0 clean, 2 invalid invocation, 3 for any detected defect or for an
inability to complete the verification. An inability to verify fails; it never
passes. There is no --json envelope, because nothing consumes one.

This is an EARLY EXIT, not a gate with unique coverage. bin/cctally-preflight-test
runs the same checks inside the suite, which is what makes
CCTALLY_SKIP_PREFLIGHT=1 safe: skipping removes a duplicate rather than
removing coverage. A gate that can fail the operation it guards turns a
detector into an outage.

No wall-clock ASSERTION exists anywhere for this tool: no duration is compared
against a ceiling, reported, or tuned, and no outcome depends on how long a
check took. It is fast because it parses and does nothing else, and a duration
ceiling on a shared, contended box is the defect class #529 S3 has just
removed.

Wall-clock BOUNDS do exist, and they are not that thing (#630 S3, #641, #655).
EVERY subprocess this tool starts carries one, and each is a hang bound: it
decides an outcome only when the subprocess never returns at all, which is the
case that turns the pre-flight into the outage it exists to prevent. There are
two of them, named separately because they bound materially different work:
`_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` on each `-n` parse, and `_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`
on the bash version probe, the ownership validator, the runtime-budget loader
and the aggregator in plan mode. Both are 60 today.

A third, `_SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS`, is an AGGREGATE bound over check 2
rather than a per-call one, because a per-call bound stops one stuck
interpreter and does not stop hundreds of them. It gates whether the next parse
is launched and never shortens a launched one; no path in this tool computes a
timeout from the time remaining, which would launch a healthy parse with a
fractional bound near the deadline and report it as a defect in the file.

The margin is what separates a bound from a ceiling. Plan mode measures 0.16 to
0.24 s against 60 — roughly 300x — on a path that takes no lock, opens no
socket and sleeps nowhere; the ceiling #529 S3 removed sat at about 2.3x and
reddened release-stamp runs on a contended box. A bound no honest run can
approach records nothing about load; a ceiling a slow box can cross records
load and calls it a defect.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import os
import pathlib
import subprocess
import sys
import time

# Check 1 compiles without writing bytecode, and this import must not undo
# that for the tool's own directory. An ordinary import of a bin/ sibling has
# CPython write bin/__pycache__/_lib_harness_durations.<tag>.pyc into the tree
# under test, which is a side effect in a tool whose whole contract is that it
# only reads. Set before the import, because the flag is read at import time.
sys.dont_write_bytecode = True

# The duration-table contract has ONE spelling, shared with the dispatch
# scheduler in bin/cctally-test-all. Resolved from this script's own directory
# rather than through an ambient PYTHONPATH, and deliberately from the tool's
# own tree rather than from --repo-root: the contract belongs to the binary
# doing the checking, not to the tree being checked.
_BIN_DIR = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(_BIN_DIR))


class _SiblingUnavailable(Exception):
    """A bin/ sibling one of the checks needs could not be loaded."""


# Every sibling below is loaded INSIDE the check that uses it, never at module
# scope. A module-scope load turns a missing or broken sibling into an
# unhandled traceback and exit 1 — a code outside this tool's taxonomy, and the
# opposite of the contract stated above, which is that an inability to complete
# a check is a reported defect at exit 3. It also crashed `--help`, which needs
# none of these modules to answer. bin/_cctally_release.py wraps its copy of
# the same lint load for the same reason.
_loaded: dict[str, object] = {}


def _sibling(name: str):
    """Import a bin/ sibling module by name, or raise _SiblingUnavailable.

    Resolved from this script's own directory rather than through an ambient
    PYTHONPATH, and deliberately from the tool's own tree rather than from
    --repo-root: the contract belongs to the binary doing the checking, not to
    the tree being checked.
    """
    if name not in _loaded:
        try:
            _loaded[name] = importlib.import_module(name)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - reported, never raised out
            raise _SiblingUnavailable(
                "%s could not be loaded from %s: %s" % (name, _BIN_DIR, exc)
            ) from exc
    return _loaded[name]


def _changelog_lint() -> object:
    """bin/cctally-changelog-lint as a module, or raise _SiblingUnavailable.

    Check 7 calls the pure kernel directly, but the kernel takes both halves of
    its path axis as INJECTED predicates, and those two are I/O glue: a
    classifier loaded from the tree's mirror data, and an existence predicate
    read from the repository index. They already exist, once, inside the lint
    executable. This loads that executable as a module and reuses them rather
    than writing a second copy, because a gate that disagreed with the lint a
    contributor runs by hand would be worse than no gate. The file is
    EXTENSIONLESS, so it cannot be imported by name; its module body only sets
    flags and imports the kernel, and its own main() is guarded.
    """
    key = "cctally-changelog-lint"
    if key not in _loaded:
        path = _BIN_DIR / key
        try:
            loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(
                "_cctally_preflight_changelog_lint", str(path)
            )
            module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(
                importlib.util.spec_from_loader(
                    "_cctally_preflight_changelog_lint", loader
                )
            )
            loader.exec_module(module)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - reported, never raised out
            raise _SiblingUnavailable(
                "%s could not be loaded: %s" % (path, exc)
            ) from exc
        _loaded[key] = module
    return _loaded[key]

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_USAGE = 2
EXIT_DEFECT = 3

_OWNERS = "cctally-test-owners"


def _shebang(path: pathlib.Path) -> str:
    try:
        with path.open("rb") as handle:
            first = handle.readline(512)
    except OSError:
        return ""
    if not first.startswith(b"#!"):
        return ""
    return first.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()


def classify(path: pathlib.Path) -> str:
    """``"python"``, ``"shell"`` or ``""`` for one file in the tree."""
    shebang = _shebang(path)
    if path.suffix == ".py" or "python" in shebang:
        return "python"
    if path.suffix == ".sh":
        return "shell"
    if shebang:
        # The interpreter word, so `#!/usr/bin/env bash` and `#!/bin/sh` are
        # both recognised while `#!/usr/bin/env node` is not.
        word = shebang.replace("#!", "").split()
        for token in reversed(word):
            base = os.path.basename(token)
            if base in ("bash", "sh", "dash", "ksh", "zsh"):
                return "shell"
    return ""


def shell_interpreter(path: pathlib.Path) -> str:
    """`bash -n` or `sh -n`, chosen from the file's OWN shebang.

    A `.sh` file with no shebang is a sourced fragment: every one of them in
    this tree is sourced by a bash harness, so bash is the interpreter that
    will actually parse it and the one whose grammar must accept it.
    """
    shebang = _shebang(path)
    for token in reversed(shebang.replace("#!", "").split()):
        base = os.path.basename(token)
        if base in ("sh", "dash"):
            return "sh"
        if base in ("bash", "ksh", "zsh"):
            return "bash"
    return "bash"


def check_python(
    root: pathlib.Path, subjects: list[pathlib.Path] | None = None
) -> list[str]:
    """Compile every Python subject in the tree without writing bytecode.

    ``subjects`` MUST be `_subjects_by_kind(root)["python"]` for the same
    ``root``, because ``root`` is then read for nothing else and a list taken
    from a different tree would be checked in silence. `run` supplies one,
    because `check_python` and `check_shell` would otherwise walk and classify
    the whole tree once each: two traversals, and every file classified twice
    where one pass answers both checks. `classify` opens every file it is
    given, so that cost is real. When the argument is omitted the check
    enumerates its own subjects, which is what keeps `check_python(root)` a
    working call for the harness and for the Linux observation.

    No count is written here, for the reason given at
    `_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`: this file ships to the public mirror, whose tree
    is a subset with a different subject set. The measured figures are in the
    maintainer's session record.
    """
    problems: list[str] = []
    if subjects is None:
        subjects = iter_python_subjects(root)
    for path in subjects:
        try:
            source = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
            problems.append("%s could not be read as UTF-8 source: %s" % (path, exc))
            continue
        try:
            compile(source, str(path), "exec", dont_inherit=True)
        except SyntaxError as exc:
            problems.append("%s:%s: %s" % (path, exc.lineno, exc.msg))
        except ValueError as exc:
            problems.append("%s: %s" % (path, exc))
    return problems


# Directory basenames pruned wherever they appear, and repository-relative
# directory paths pruned at their own location, with the tracked trees that sit
# INSIDE a pruned path re-included. The universe of this check is source, and
# the walk is a pruned filesystem walk rather than `git ls-files` because
# pre-flight must not require a git repository: bin/cctally-test-remote
# materializes the working tree onto a runner, and a materialized copy is
# exactly the tree this check most needs to cover.
#
# .cctally-remote-state is the one entry that is not merely an optimisation.
# It is a runner-owned root, and provisioning clones a SEPARATE repository
# beneath it; bin/cctally-preflight-test runs this tool against the real working
# tree, so an unpruned walk would report another project's shell files as this
# repository's defects.
_PRUNE_DIR_NAMES = frozenset({
    ".git", ".worktrees", "node_modules", "__pycache__", ".venv",
    ".cctally-remote-state", ".wrangler", ".superpowers", ".playwright-mcp",
})
_PRUNE_DIR_PATHS = ("dashboard/static", ".agentmem/local", ".claude", ".codex")
# .claude/ and .codex/ are mirror-private, so the public tree carries neither,
# and that is exactly why naming them here is safe rather than a dependency:
# these constants only decide whether to DESCEND into a directory the walk has
# already found. On a tree without them os.walk never yields one and the names
# are never compared against anything, so absence is tolerated by construction,
# which is the condition the waiver below exists for. They are named at all
# because `.gitignore` is default-deny over both trees with exactly these
# re-included, and .claude/skills holds a shell subject the walk must reach.
_KEEP_DIR_PATHS = (  # mirror-private-ok
    ".claude/skills", ".claude/agents", ".codex/agents",
)


def _kept(rel: str) -> bool:
    """True when ``rel`` sits inside a tree re-included from a pruned path."""
    return any(rel == keep or rel.startswith(keep + "/") for keep in _KEEP_DIR_PATHS)


def iter_source_files(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[pathlib.Path]:
    """Every non-pruned regular file in the tree, in a stable order.

    The walk is a pruned filesystem walk rather than `git ls-files` because
    pre-flight must not require a git repository: bin/cctally-test-remote
    materializes the working tree onto a runner, and a materialized copy is
    exactly the tree this check most needs to cover.

    Classification is deliberately NOT applied here, so that one traversal can
    be classified once and partitioned by `_subjects_by_kind`. That is what
    makes a single walk POSSIBLE; it does not by itself make one happen, and an
    earlier draft of this docstring claimed it did. Whether the tree is walked
    once is decided by the caller: `run` partitions once and hands each check
    its own list, while `iter_shell_subjects` and `iter_python_subjects` each
    perform their own walk when they are called on their own. Those two are
    kept as standalone seams for callers outside `run` — the harness's
    walk-prune-matrix case and the Linux observation both call them directly —
    and the cost is real, because `classify` opens every file it is given.
    """
    files: list[pathlib.Path] = []
    for dirpath, dirnames, filenames in os.walk(root):
        rel = os.path.relpath(dirpath, root)
        rel = "" if rel == "." else rel.replace(os.sep, "/")
        keep_dirs = []
        for name in dirnames:
            child = name if not rel else rel + "/" + name
            if name in _PRUNE_DIR_NAMES:
                continue
            if child in _PRUNE_DIR_PATHS and not _kept(child):
                # Descend anyway when a re-included tree lives beneath, so
                # .claude/skills survives a pruned .claude.
                if not any(keep.startswith(child + "/") for keep in _KEEP_DIR_PATHS):
                    continue
            keep_dirs.append(name)
        dirnames[:] = sorted(keep_dirs)
        for name in sorted(filenames):
            relfile = name if not rel else rel + "/" + name
            if not _kept(relfile) and any(
                relfile.startswith(pruned + "/") for pruned in _PRUNE_DIR_PATHS
            ):
                continue
            path = root / relfile
            if path.is_file():
                files.append(path)
    return files


def _subjects_by_kind(root: pathlib.Path) -> dict[str, list[pathlib.Path]]:
    """One walk, one classification pass, partitioned by kind."""
    by_kind: dict[str, list[pathlib.Path]] = {"shell": [], "python": []}
    for path in iter_source_files(root):
        kind = classify(path)
        if kind in by_kind:
            by_kind[kind].append(path)
    return by_kind


def iter_shell_subjects(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[pathlib.Path]:
    """Every shell subject in the tree, in a stable order.

    Classified exactly as ``classify`` classifies one file, so a `.sh` file
    with no shebang is included: ``shell_interpreter``'s contract is that such
    a file is a bash-sourced fragment, and shebangless fragments are a
    recurring shape in this tree rather than an anomaly. A shebang-keyed
    enumeration would silently exclude every one of them.

    No count is written here, for the reason given at
    `_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`: this file ships to the public mirror, whose tree
    is a subset with a different subject set.
    """
    return _subjects_by_kind(root)["shell"]


def iter_python_subjects(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[pathlib.Path]:
    """Every Python subject in the tree, in a stable order.

    Repository-wide rather than bin/-only (#655). check_shell has been
    tree-wide since #641, and the asymmetry meant a Python syntax error
    anywhere outside bin/ was invisible to the pre-flight.
    """
    return _subjects_by_kind(root)["python"]


_LEGACY_BASH_ENV = "CCTALLY_PREFLIGHT_BASH32"

# A hang bound on every `-n` parse, matching the one check 5 puts on the
# aggregator. An interpreter that never returns would hang the pre-flight, and a
# hung pre-flight is the outage class this tool's docstring says it exists to
# avoid. It is generous because these are parses and nothing else: the primary
# leg sweeps every shell subject in the tree, while the legacy bash-3.x leg
# sweeps only the bash-interpreted ones; the honest full shell check takes a few
# seconds, so a single subject that reaches this bound is stuck rather than
# slow. A timeout is reported as a finding, so the exit taxonomy still answers 3
# rather than propagating an exception.
#
# Deliberately no subject COUNT here (#655). This file ships to the public
# mirror, whose tree is a subset with a different subject set, so a count
# asserted here is false there — and it goes stale in this tree on any added
# file, which happened during the very session that corrected this comment. The
# exact counts belong in the maintainer's remote-testing reference.
_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 60

# The same hang bound, for the subprocesses that are NOT parses: the bash
# version probe, the ownership validator, the runtime-budget validator and the
# aggregator in plan mode. Deliberately a SEPARATE name from the parse bound
# even though the values match today. They bound materially different work —
# one interpreter parsing one file, versus a Python validator or the aggregator
# — so a later measurement that moves one must not silently move the other.
_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 60

# An AGGREGATE bound over the whole shell check, distinct from the per-call one.
# The per-call bound stops ONE stuck interpreter; it does not stop hundreds of
# them, whose aggregate worst case on this tree is roughly twelve hours.
#
# It gates only whether the NEXT parse is LAUNCHED. It never shortens a parse
# that has been launched, and no code path anywhere computes a per-call timeout
# from the time remaining. An earlier design capped each call at
# min(per_call, remaining), and that is wrong in exactly the way this tool's
# docstring forbids: as remaining approaches zero a perfectly healthy parse is
# launched with a fractional timeout, times out, and is reported as a defect in
# the file. A bound that fails a healthy subject because the box was slow is a
# ceiling, which is the class #529 S3 removed.
#
# 600 against an honest full check of about 5.8 seconds is roughly 100x. The
# ceiling #529 S3 removed sat at about 2.3x.
_SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 600

# A minimal reproduction of the defect class the legacy leg exists to see: a
# `case` pattern's closing paren ends the enclosing `$( … )` early, because
# bash 3.2 scans a command substitution by counting characters rather than by
# parsing it. Fed to the interpreter on STDIN and never written to disk —
# pre-flight's contract is that it only reads, to the point of setting
# sys.dont_write_bytecode so its own imports leave no __pycache__ in the tree
# under test, and a temporary control file would break that for no benefit.
# Measured: /bin/bash 3.2 -n exits 2 on this, bash 5.x -n exits 0.
_LEGACY_CONTROL = "x=$(case a in\nb) echo b ;;\nesac)\n"


def _bash_major(interpreter: str) -> tuple[str, str]:
    """``(major, "")`` for a bash-family interpreter, or ``("", reason)``."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [interpreter, "-c", 'printf %s "${BASH_VERSINFO[0]}"'],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return "", "%s could not be run: %s" % (interpreter, exc)
    major = (proc.stdout or "").strip()
    if proc.returncode != 0 or not major.isdigit():
        return "", "%s did not report a bash major version" % interpreter
    return major, ""


def resolve_legacy_bash() -> tuple[str, list[str], list[str]]:
    """``(interpreter, problems, notes)`` for the bash 3.x floor leg.

    Resolution order: the path in CCTALLY_PREFLIGHT_BASH32 when that variable is
    set and non-empty; otherwise /bin/bash when its BASH_VERSINFO[0] is 3;
    otherwise no legacy interpreter is available.

    An absent interpreter is a STATED skip and not a finding, because a Linux
    container legitimately has no 3.x bash. A PIN that is not major version 3 is
    a finding, because the pin is a claim about what the interpreter is and a
    false claim is an operator error rather than an absent capability.

    A probe that FAILED is a finding on both paths (#655). Absence, and a
    version that is simply not 3, remain the stated skip they have always been;
    a /bin/bash that hangs or cannot be run is neither of those, and reporting
    it as an absent interpreter would drop the floor leg without a word.
    """
    pinned = os.environ.get(_LEGACY_BASH_ENV, "").strip()
    if pinned:
        major, reason = _bash_major(pinned)
        if reason:
            return "", ["%s=%s cannot be used as the bash 3.x floor: %s"
                        % (_LEGACY_BASH_ENV, pinned, reason)], []
        if major != "3":
            return "", ["%s=%s reports bash major version %s, not 3, so it cannot "
                        "stand in for the 3.2 floor — pin a 3.x bash or unset the "
                        "variable" % (_LEGACY_BASH_ENV, pinned, major)], []
        return pinned, [], []
    absent = [
        "no bash 3.x interpreter is available, so the declared bash 3.2 floor was "
        "NOT checked on this host; set %s to one to enable that leg"
        % _LEGACY_BASH_ENV
    ]
    probe = "/bin/bash"
    if not os.path.exists(probe):
        # EXISTENCE, not executability. os.access(probe, os.X_OK) was the test
        # here, which sent a /bin/bash that exists but cannot be executed down
        # this path and reported it as an absent interpreter — the same
        # fail-open T11 removes one line below, reintroduced by the guard that
        # precedes it. Present-but-unrunnable is not absence: it reaches
        # _bash_major, whose PermissionError is an OSError and becomes the
        # finding T11 requires. Absence is the Linux-container case this note
        # exists for, and nothing is probed there, so nothing can fail.
        return "", [], absent
    major, reason = _bash_major(probe)
    if major == "3":
        return probe, [], []
    if reason:
        # A probe that FAILED is not an interpreter that is absent. This return
        # used to discard the reason, so a hung or unrunnable /bin/bash was
        # reported as "no bash 3.x available" — a fail-open path on the exact
        # leg #641 added, which would silently drop the floor leg on the very
        # host that cannot answer for it. A version that is simply not 3 stays
        # the stated skip it has always been.
        return "", ["the bash 3.x floor could not be resolved: %s" % reason], []
    return "", [], absent


def _legacy_parser_is_blind(interpreter: str) -> list[str]:
    """A parser that accepts the known-bad control asserts nothing."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [interpreter, "-n"], input=_LEGACY_CONTROL,
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # Ahead of the broader clause below, and worded like the two timeout
        # findings in check_shell: the leg, the invocation, the bound and the
        # operation. The generic wording names none of them, so a hung
        # interpreter here read as an unspecified subprocess failure.
        return ["the bash 3.x floor cannot be checked: %s -n did not return "
                "within %d seconds while parsing the known-bad control"
                % (interpreter, _PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)]
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return ["the bash 3.x floor cannot be checked: %s could not parse the "
                "known-bad control: %s" % (interpreter, exc)]
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return ["%s ACCEPTS the known-bad bash 3.2 control, so it is blind to the "
                "defect class this leg exists to see and a green verdict from it "
                "asserts nothing — pin a real 3.x bash in %s, or unset it"
                % (interpreter, _LEGACY_BASH_ENV)]
    return []


def check_shell(
    root: pathlib.Path, notes: list[str],
    subjects: list[pathlib.Path] | None = None,
) -> list[str]:
    """Syntax-check every shell subject in the TREE, on three legs.

    ``subjects`` lets the caller supply an already-partitioned subject list, on
    the same terms as `check_python`: it MUST be
    `_subjects_by_kind(root)["shell"]` for the same ``root``, because ``root``
    is read for nothing else once it is supplied. `run` classifies the tree
    once and hands each check its half, and an omitted argument means enumerate
    here. See `check_python` for the cost that motivates it.

    ``notes`` is REQUIRED rather than defaulted. The floor leg's skip is stated
    by appending to it, and a default would let a caller drop that statement by
    omitting one argument — which is the silent skip this check forbids.

    The primary parse uses the interpreter the file's own shebang names. Two
    further legs exist because the primary one, alone, missed three real
    defects in this repository:

      * A non-empty stderr at exit 0 is a finding. bash 5 reports
        `unterminated here-document` this way, and a return-code-only reading
        has never reported it. This leg needs no 3.x interpreter and therefore
        works on Linux.
      * Every subject `shell_interpreter` calls bash is parsed a second time by
        a bash 3.x interpreter, which is the floor this repository declares.
        Homebrew bash 5 answers `bash` on PATH here and on both LAN runners, so
        the declared floor was never actually exercised.

    The sweep STOPS on either of two conditions, and neither shortens a parse
    that has already been launched (#655). `_SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS`
    gates whether the next parse is launched at all, and a parse that exhausts
    its own full `_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` stops the sweep as well, because one
    stuck interpreter means this tree is not verifiable on this host. When the
    sweep stops, a finding states how many subjects each leg left unchecked,
    rather than a short list of per-subject findings reading as a clean tree.
    """
    problems: list[str] = []
    legacy, legacy_problems, legacy_notes = resolve_legacy_bash()
    problems += legacy_problems
    notes += legacy_notes
    if legacy:
        blind = _legacy_parser_is_blind(legacy)
        if blind:
            problems += blind
            legacy = ""
    if subjects is None:
        subjects = iter_shell_subjects(root)
    # T1 says the aggregate clock starts at check_shell ENTRY. It starts here
    # instead, after the legacy interpreter has been resolved, after the
    # blind-parser control parse, and after the subject enumeration. Each of
    # those carries its own bound already — the two probes are bounded
    # subprocesses and the walk runs no subprocess at all — so excluding them
    # gives the sweep its full deadline rather than a deadline the preamble has
    # already spent part of. The property T1 exists for is preserved: what the
    # clock gates is whether the next PARSE is launched.
    started = time.monotonic()
    stop_reason = ""
    # The first subject index whose leg was never resolved. Kept separately per
    # leg because a legacy parse that hangs has already had its primary parse
    # answered, and reporting that subject as an unchecked PRIMARY would
    # overstate what was left unverified.
    primary_stop = len(subjects)
    floor_stop = len(subjects)
    for index, path in enumerate(subjects):
        if time.monotonic() - started >= _SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS:
            # Gates whether this parse is LAUNCHED. It never shortens one that
            # has been. See _SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS.
            stop_reason = ("the aggregate %d-second deadline expired"
                           % _SHELL_CHECK_DEADLINE_SECONDS)
            primary_stop = floor_stop = index
            break
        interpreter = shell_interpreter(path)
        try:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                [interpreter, "-n", str(path)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                timeout=_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
            )
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            problems.append(
                "%s could not be syntax-checked: %s -n did not return within %d "
                "seconds" % (path, interpreter, _PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
            )
            # One interpreter that exhausts its FULL bound means this tree is
            # not verifiable on this host. The run is going to exit 3 whatever
            # follows, and continuing would spend the same bound on every
            # remaining subject to prove it again.
            stop_reason = ("%s -n exhausted its full %d-second bound on %s"
                           % (interpreter, _PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, path))
            primary_stop = floor_stop = index
            break
        except OSError as exc:
            problems.append(
                "%s could not be syntax-checked: %s -n is unavailable (%s)"
                % (path, interpreter, exc)
            )
            continue
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            detail = (proc.stderr or proc.stdout).strip().splitlines()
            problems.append(
                "%s: %s" % (path, detail[0] if detail else "%s -n exited %d"
                            % (interpreter, proc.returncode))
            )
            # A file the current interpreter already refuses does not need the
            # floor's opinion as well; one defect, one finding.
            continue
        warning = (proc.stderr or "").strip().splitlines()
        if warning:
            problems.append(
                "%s: %s -n exited 0 but wrote a diagnostic: %s"
                % (path, interpreter, warning[0])
            )
        if not legacy or interpreter != "bash":
            continue
        try:
            old = subprocess.run(
                [legacy, "-n", str(path)], capture_output=True, text=True,
                timeout=_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
            )
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            problems.append(
                "%s could not be checked against the bash 3.2 floor: %s -n did "
                "not return within %d seconds"
                % (path, legacy, _PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
            )
            stop_reason = ("%s -n exhausted its full %d-second bound on %s"
                           % (legacy, _PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, path))
            # This subject's PRIMARY parse already answered, so only the floor
            # leg is unresolved from here.
            primary_stop = index + 1
            floor_stop = index
            break
        except OSError as exc:
            problems.append(
                "%s could not be checked against the bash 3.2 floor: %s -n is "
                "unavailable (%s)" % (path, legacy, exc)
            )
            continue
        if old.returncode != 0:
            detail = (old.stderr or old.stdout).strip().splitlines()
            problems.append(
                "%s: does not parse under the declared bash 3.2 floor (%s): %s"
                % (path, legacy, detail[0] if detail else "-n exited %d" % old.returncode)
            )
    if stop_reason:
        # An incomplete sweep must never read as a clean tree. This tool's
        # contract is that an inability to verify FAILS, so what went
        # unverified is stated rather than left to be inferred from a count of
        # per-subject findings that is silently short.
        #
        # The floor tally below is an UPPER bound, not an exact count. It
        # counts every remaining bash-interpreted subject, and the floor leg is
        # reached only for a subject whose PRIMARY parse exited 0: one that
        # would have failed, or that raised OSError, is `continue`d before the
        # floor leg and would never have reached it. Overstating what went
        # unverified is the safe direction for a finding whose whole purpose is
        # to refuse a clean verdict.
        floor_unchecked = (
            sum(1 for remaining in subjects[floor_stop:]
                if shell_interpreter(remaining) == "bash") if legacy else 0
        )
        problems.append(
            "the shell syntax check did not complete (%s): %d primary "
            "subject(s) and %d bash 3.2-floor subject(s) were left unchecked; "
            "no clean shell verdict is available"
            % (stop_reason, len(subjects) - primary_stop, floor_unchecked)
        )
    return problems


def check_ownership(root: pathlib.Path, owners: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    if not owners.is_file():
        return ["%s is absent, so harness ownership cannot be verified" % owners]
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [sys.executable, str(owners), "--verify", "--repo-root", str(root)],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # This subprocess carried no bound at all until #655, while the tool's
        # own documentation said every wall-clock bound in it is a hang bound —
        # which invites the reading that every subprocess has one. A validator
        # that never returns is an inability to verify ownership, which this
        # tool reports as a defect rather than as a pass.
        return ["harness ownership could not be verified: %s did not return "
                "within %d seconds" % (owners, _SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)]
    except OSError as exc:
        return ["%s could not be run: %s" % (owners, exc)]
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return []
    lines = [ln for ln in (proc.stderr or proc.stdout).splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    if not lines:
        lines = ["%s --verify exited %d with no diagnostic" % (owners, proc.returncode)]
    return lines


def check_durations(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """The dispatch scheduler's own validation, applied as a refusal.

    Deliberately the same three calls the scheduler makes, in the same order,
    so the two can only ever disagree about the consequence.
    """
    try:
        durations = _sibling("_lib_harness_durations")
    except _SiblingUnavailable as exc:
        return ["the harness duration table cannot be validated: %s" % exc]
    manifest, error = durations.read_manifest(str(root))
    if error:
        return [error]
    text, error = durations.read_table(str(root))
    if error:
        return [error]
    _table, error = durations.validate(text, manifest)
    if error:
        return [error]
    return []


_CONTRACT_LIB = "_lib-test-contract.sh"


def check_runtime_budget(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """The committed runtime budget, validated by the parser that owns it.

    check_durations reuses the scheduler's validator by IMPORTING
    _lib_harness_durations and calling its Python API. There is no equivalent
    module here: contract_budget_load is embedded Python inside a shell library,
    so nothing can be imported and that structure cannot be reused. The real
    loader is invoked through a caught subprocess instead, which is what keeps
    the committed file validated by the same parser that validates it at
    admission and keeps the refusal vocabulary in exactly one place.

    Resolved from this script's own directory, exactly as _sibling resolves the
    Python siblings and for the same reason: the contract belongs to the binary
    doing the checking, not to the tree being checked. A library that is absent
    or that fails is an inability to complete a declared check, which this tool
    reports as a defect at exit 3 rather than as a pass.
    """
    library = _BIN_DIR / _CONTRACT_LIB
    budget = root / "tests" / "authoritative-runtime-budget.json"
    if not library.is_file():
        return [
            "the committed runtime budget cannot be validated: %s is absent, so "
            "the loader that owns the file's contract cannot be reached" % library
        ]
    script = (
        'set -uo pipefail\n'
        'source "$1" || exit 2\n'
        'contract_budget_load "$2" || { contract_print_diagnostics; exit 1; }\n'
    )
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["bash", "-c", script, "cctally-preflight", str(library), str(budget)],
            capture_output=True, text=True,
            # The same HANG bound check 5 carries, for the same reason: it
            # decides an outcome only when the subprocess never returns at all.
            timeout=_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return [
            "the committed runtime budget cannot be validated: %s could not be "
            "run: %s" % (library, exc)
        ]
    if proc.returncode == 0:
        return []
    lines = [ln.strip() for ln in (proc.stderr or proc.stdout).splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    if proc.returncode != 1 or not lines:
        return [
            "the committed runtime budget cannot be validated: %s exited %d "
            "against %s%s"
            % (library, proc.returncode, budget, (" - %s" % lines[-1]) if lines else "")
        ]
    # The loader's own diagnostic, verbatim. It already names the file and the
    # cause, and restating either here would be a second spelling of a
    # vocabulary this repository keeps in exactly one place.
    return lines


def _forced_dispatch() -> str:
    """The dispatch arm the operator asked for, or "" when they asked for none.

    Read through one helper so the tool and its harness agree on the spelling,
    and so a future third arm has one place to be added.
    """
    return os.environ.get("CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_DISPATCH", "").strip()


def check_scheduler_plan(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """The dispatch decision, asked for rather than inferred.

    check_durations reads the three files the scheduler reads, so it can only
    prove the table is well formed. It cannot see the run-time conditions that
    make the scheduler fall back anyway and stay silent about it: no python3 on
    PATH, a python3 that answers non-zero, _lib_harness_durations unimportable
    from <root>/bin, or a broken shell-side capture. Plan mode reports the
    decision itself, exits before admission and before any harness launch, and
    is the aggregator's own documented dry run — so asking it is the cheapest
    honest way to check the half of §3's check 4 that file validation cannot.

    A tree carrying NO aggregator has no plan to report and is not faulted:
    this tool's synthetic-fixture callers are that shape, and a real checkout
    that lost bin/cctally-test-all has no round trip to pre-flight. A tree
    carrying one that lost its executable bit is the opposite case and is
    faulted here, because nothing else sees it: bin/cctally-test-owners
    --verify checks the mode bit of every manifest harness, and the aggregator
    is deliberately not one of them, so conflating the two answered `clean` on
    a tree whose pool could not start.
    """
    aggregator = root / "bin" / "cctally-test-all"
    if not aggregator.exists():
        return []
    if not os.access(aggregator, os.X_OK):
        return [
            "%s is present but not executable, so the dispatch plan cannot be "
            "read — chmod +x it" % aggregator
        ]
    environment = dict(os.environ)
    environment["CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_PLAN"] = "1"
    # Under the suite this tool runs as a harness, and the aggregator's own
    # environment reaches it. CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_OUTCOME_FILE is set for every
    # authoritative run, and the aggregator refuses to combine it with plan
    # mode (exit 2, "a plan is never an authoritative outcome"), so leaving it
    # in place would fail this check on precisely the runs that matter most.
    environment.pop("CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_OUTCOME_FILE", None)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [str(aggregator)],
            cwd=str(root),
            env=environment,
            capture_output=True,
            text=True,
            # This tool is seconds-scale by contract. The timeout is not a
            # performance assertion — it is not compared against, reported, or
            # tuned — it is the bound that keeps a hung aggregator from turning
            # a pre-flight into the outage it exists to prevent.
            timeout=_SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError) as exc:
        return ["the dispatch plan could not be read from %s: %s" % (aggregator, exc)]
    detail = (proc.stderr or "").strip().splitlines()
    trailer = (" — %s" % detail[-1]) if detail else ""
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return [
            "%s plan mode exited %d, so the dispatch decision cannot be read%s"
            % (aggregator, proc.returncode, trailer)
        ]
    mode = None
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("scheduler="):
            mode = line.split("=", 1)[1].strip()
    if mode is None:
        return [
            "%s plan mode printed no scheduler= line, so the dispatch decision "
            "cannot be read" % aggregator
        ]
    # The expected mode follows the operator's own explicit choice. This check
    # exists to catch an UNINTENDED fallback, and #630 S3's measurement
    # programme runs its control arm with CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_DISPATCH=report set.
    # Plan mode is a subprocess and inherits the environment, so without this
    # the wrapper would refuse every control-arm run for doing exactly what it
    # was told. The table itself is still validated independently by
    # check_durations, so forcing the control arm hides no table defect.
    expected = "report-forced" if _forced_dispatch() == "report" else "lpt"
    if mode != expected:
        if expected == "report-forced":
            return [
                "CCTALLY_TEST_ALL_DISPATCH=report was set but the dispatch "
                "scheduler reports scheduler=%s, so this run is not the control "
                "arm it was asked for%s" % (mode, trailer)
            ]
        return [
            "the dispatch scheduler reports scheduler=%s, so the pool would run "
            "in report order rather than longest-first%s" % (mode, trailer)
        ]
    return []


def check_visibility(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """contract_admit_visibility, run locally.

    Private profile only: .mirror-allowlist excludes itself, so its presence is
    exactly the private discriminator, and the public tree ships neither that
    file nor the classifier.
    """
    allowlist = root / ".mirror-allowlist"
    if not allowlist.is_file():
        return []
    classifier = root / ".githooks" / "_match.py"
    if not classifier.is_file():
        return [
            "this tree is the PRIVATE profile (.mirror-allowlist is present) but "
            "the allowlist classifier %s is absent, so the declared visibility of "
            "every manifest row cannot be checked — restore it, or remove "
            ".mirror-allowlist if this tree is meant to be the public subset"
            % classifier
        ]
    try:
        durations = _sibling("_lib_harness_durations")
    except _SiblingUnavailable as exc:
        return ["declared visibility cannot be checked: %s" % exc]
    manifest, error = durations.read_manifest(str(root))
    if error:
        return [error]
    try:
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
            "_cctally_preflight_match", str(classifier)
        )
        module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        spec.loader.exec_module(module)
        text = allowlist.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        paths = ["bin/cctally-%s-test" % name for name, _ in manifest]
        public = set(module.classify(paths, allowlist_text=text)["public"])
    except Exception as exc:  # the classifier is arbitrary local code
        return ["the allowlist classifier %s could not be run: %s" % (classifier, exc)]
    problems: list[str] = []
    for (name, declared), path in zip(manifest, paths):
        actual = "public" if path in public else "private"
        if actual != declared:
            problems.append(
                "manifest row '%s' declares visibility '%s' but .githooks/_match.py "
                "classifies %s as '%s' — fix the row or fix .mirror-allowlist"
                % (name, declared, path, actual)
            )
    return problems


def check_changelog(root: pathlib.Path) -> list[str]:
    """CHANGELOG.md entries must meet the policy in docs/changelog-policy.md.

    Both halves of the kernel's path axis are supplied where this tree carries
    what each needs. The mirror classifier answers whether a cited path would
    be published and is absent on the public mirror, where the kernel records
    that the layer did not run rather than reporting a clean pass over a
    population it could not see; the existence predicate answers whether this
    tree carries the path and is always supplied.

    An inability to check is a defect, not a pass: a classifier whose data is
    present but which cannot be loaded, an absent or broken lint executable,
    and an unreadable changelog all report a problem rather than an empty list.
    """
    target = root / "CHANGELOG.md"
    try:
        text = target.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as exc:
        return ["cannot read %s: %s" % (target, exc)]
    try:
        lint = _changelog_lint()
        policy = _sibling("_lib_changelog_policy")
        exists, _source = lint._tree_exists(root)
        classify = lint._load_classify(root)
        report = policy.check_text(text, classify=classify, exists=exists)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - see docstring
        return [
            "CHANGELOG.md could not be checked against the user-facing policy: %s"
            % exc
        ]
    return [
        "CHANGELOG.md:%d: %s: %s (see docs/changelog-policy.md)"
        % (finding.line, finding.rule, finding.detail)
        for finding in report.findings
    ]


def run(root: pathlib.Path) -> tuple[int, list[str], list[str]]:
    """``(exit code, problems, notes)``.

    A NOTE is a leg that could not run for a legitimate reason — today, only an
    absent bash 3.x interpreter. It is stated rather than silent, because a leg
    that can vanish without a word is a leg nobody notices has stopped running.
    It is not a finding and does not change the exit code.
    """
    notes: list[str] = []
    bin_dir = root / "bin"
    if not bin_dir.is_dir():
        return EXIT_DEFECT, [
            "%s has no bin/ directory, so nothing can be checked" % root
        ], notes
    # One walk, one classification pass, both halves handed on. Calling
    # check_python(root) and check_shell(root, notes) here instead walks the
    # tree twice and classifies every file twice. No count is written here,
    # for the reason given at `_PARSE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`.
    #
    # This also enumerates the SHELL subjects before check_python compiles its
    # own, where the shell walk previously ran after check_python returned.
    # That widens the window between a subject's is_file() at walk time and its
    # `bash -n` at parse time, so a file deleted inside it is reported as a
    # defect rather than skipped. The harness's real-tree case already assumes
    # a quiescent estate for the whole run; this widens that assumption, it
    # does not introduce it.
    by_kind = _subjects_by_kind(root)
    problems = check_python(root, by_kind["python"])
    problems += check_shell(root, notes, by_kind["shell"])
    problems += check_ownership(root, pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent / _OWNERS)
    problems += check_durations(root)
    problems += check_scheduler_plan(root)
    problems += check_visibility(root)
    problems += check_changelog(root)
    problems += check_runtime_budget(root)
    return (EXIT_DEFECT if problems else EXIT_OK), problems, notes


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="cctally-preflight",
        description=(
            "Compile every Python file in the repository, syntax-check every "
            "shell subject in the repository against its own interpreter and against "
            "the declared bash 3.2 floor, verify harness ownership, validate "
            "the harness duration table, confirm the dispatch scheduler is "
            "armed, check declared "
            "visibility against the mirror allowlist, check CHANGELOG.md "
            "against the user-facing entry policy, and validate the committed "
            "runtime budget through its own loader — before a remote round trip."
        ),
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--repo-root", metavar="DIR",
        help="check this tree instead of the one containing this script",
    )
    try:
        args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    except SystemExit as exc:
        return EXIT_OK if exc.code == 0 else EXIT_USAGE

    root = pathlib.Path(args.repo_root) if args.repo_root \
        else pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
    if not root.is_dir():
        sys.stderr.write("preflight: %s is not a directory\n" % root)
        return EXIT_USAGE

    code, problems, notes = run(root)
    for note in notes:
        sys.stderr.write("preflight: note: %s\n" % note)
    for problem in problems:
        sys.stderr.write("preflight: %s\n" % problem)
    if code == EXIT_OK:
        sys.stdout.write("preflight: clean\n")
    else:
        sys.stderr.write(
            "preflight: %d problem(s); fix them before the round trip, or set "
            "CCTALLY_SKIP_PREFLIGHT=1 if the pre-flight itself is what is "
            "broken (the suite runs the same checks)\n" % len(problems)
        )
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
